      "text": "Let's solve the problem step-by-step.\n\n1. We know that Ace and Zeppo are already chosen for the starting lineup.\n2. We need to choose 3 more players from the remaining 10 players to complete the starting lineup.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 players from 10 is given by the combination formula \\(\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\), where \\(n\\) is the total number of items to choose from, and \\(k\\) is the number of items to be chosen.\n\nFor this problem, \\(n = 10\\) and \\(k = 3\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3!7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 120\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of different starting lineups possible is 120. We'll confirm this by using Python code.\n\nLet's write the Python code to verify this calculation.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the number of ways to choose 3 players from 10\nn = 10\nk = 3\nnum_lineups = math.comb(n, k)\nprint(num_lineups)\n```\n```output\n120\n```\nThe Python code confirms that the number of different starting lineups is indeed \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
